<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="pectin degradation III" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-1081" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: 1-4-alpha-D-galacturonosyl &quot;Homogalacturonan&quot;| (HGA) is a polysaccharide consisting of &amp;alpha;-1,4-linked |FRAME: D-GALACTURONATE| units. HGA accounts for up to 60% of the |FRAME: Pectin pectin| found in primary cell walls of all plants. Many of the carboxyl groups of HGA in pectin are methyl-esterified at position 6. The regions of pectin with an HGA backbone are called &quot;smooth&quot; regions, as opposed to the &quot;hairy&quot; regions, which consist of a |FRAME: Xylogalacturonan xylogalacturonan| or |FRAME: Rhamnogalacturonans-I &quot;rhamnogalacturonan type I&quot;| backbones. Degradation of HGA occurs under two rather different situations. In the first one, indigenous plant enzymes disassemble the polymer as part of normal growth and development. In the second, fungi and bacteria degrade the polymer, either as a part of pathogenic attack, or simply as part of the degradation of dead plant matter. Even though pectin is degraded for very different purposes in these two different scenarioes, the plant, fungal, and bacterial enbzymes involved are rather similar. The enzymes that cleave the main chain can be conceptually divided into hydrolases and lyases. While hydrolases release either monomers or oligomers of D-glucuronate, lyases cleave the backbone by &amp;beta;-elimination, resulting in the formation of a &amp;Delta;4,5-unsaturated non-reducing end. Either type of enzyme can be endo-cleaving (attacking at random positions in the middle of the chain) or exo-cleaving enzyme (attacking either at, or close to, one of the ends of the molecule). Most exo-cleaving enzymes are specific for the non-reducing end. &lt;b&gt;Degradation by plant enzymes&lt;/b&gt; The pectin network is systematically disassembled during many stages of plant development, especially those that require cell separation including fruit ripening, organ abscission, pod and anther dehiscence, and pollen grain maturation. Two key enzymes in the homogalacturonan disassembling process are |FRAME: EC-3.1.1.11|, and |FRAME: EC-3.2.1.15|. The pectinesterase removes the methyl groups in the form of |FRAME: METOH|, preparing de-esterified domains of homogalacturonan |CITS: [8018878][11943159][12033812]|. The de-esterified domains are suitable substrates for the endo-polygalacturonase |CITS: [6489331][1392611][8208850][7972500][8029352]| and other pectinolytic enzymes such as |FRAME: EC-3.2.1.67| |CITS: [8433375]|. These de-esterified domains can also be cross-linked with calcium, resulting in gel formation that contributes to intercellular adhesion. Pectate lyases have also been documented in plants |CITS: [19144526]|. Plant genomes contain a large number of genes that encode pectate lyase-like proteins (26 such genes are present in the Arabidopsis genome), although they haven&apos;t been biochemically characterized yet |CITS: [17447910][12324535]|. &lt;b&gt;Degradation by fungi and bacteria&lt;/b&gt; Fungal and bacterial enzymes involved in HGA degradation include |FRAME: EC-3.1.1.11|, |FRAME: EC-4.2.2.2|, |FRAME: EC-4.2.2.9|, |FRAME: EC-4.2.2.10|, |FRAME: EC-3.2.1.15|, |FRAME: EC-3.2.1.67|, and |FRAME: EC-3.2.1.82|. Many organisms have many different paralogs of a particular enzyme, each somewhat different in its preferences and serving a somewhat different role (for example, |FRAME: TAX-5061| has seven different variants of |FRAME: EC-3.2.1.15|). The combined action of these many enzymes result in an efficient degradation process. Some of the enzymes require that the backbone has little or no methyl-esterification (e.g. |FRAME: EC-4.2.2.2|), while others require methyl-esterification for activity (e.g. |FRAME: EC-4.2.2.10|). Pectinesterases that remove the methyl groups have been purified from both fungi |CITS: [1937044][8920970][10677368]| and bacteria |CITS: [2837615][8370537][10872083]|. A pectin molecule that has been de-esterified is called |FRAME: PECTATE|. Two types of endo-lyases that act on HGA are |FRAME: EC-4.2.2.10|, which prefers substrates with a high degree of methylesterification, and |FRAME: EC-4.2.2.2|, which prefers those with a low degree of esterification. A clearer distinction between these two enzyme regards their dependence on Ca&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt; ions: while pectate lyases have an absolute requirement for Ca&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;, pectin lyases do not require it |CITS: [9195887]|. Six pectin lyase genes and one pectate lyase gene have been identified in |FRAME: TAX-5061| |CITS: [2535502][7788717][11112543]|. Another type of lyase, reported only from bacteria, is |FRAME: EC-4.2.2.9|, which cleaves an unsaturated disaccharide from the non-reducing end |CITS: [14188174][10049400][10383957]|. Yet another lyase, |FRAME: EC-4.2.2.27|, described from the fungus |FRAME: TAX-5060|, cleaves an unsaturated monosaccharide from the non-reducing end of methyl-esterified susbstrates |CITS: [25610636]|. The main hydrolases that act on the HGA backbone are |FRAME: EC-3.2.1.15|, which cleaves randomly within the pectin smooth region, forming shorter oligosaccharides |CITS: [2535502][2331322][10675564]|, and |FRAME: EC-3.2.1.67|, an exo-polygalacturonase that removes monosaccharides from the non-reducing ends of these oligosaccharides |CITS:[Hata84][24414660][Beldman96][8856078]|. Another hydrolase, reported so far only from bacteria, is |FRAME: EC-3.2.1.82|, which cleaves disaccharides off the non-reducing end |CITS: [5661621][2168372]|. No less than seven endopolygalacturonases have been characterized from |FRAME: TAX-5061|. These enzymes differ in their specific activity (varying from 25 to 4,000 U/mg), sensitivity to methylation of the substrate |CITS: [10092840][10601263][10642523][10675564]|, and mode of action. Endopolygalacturonases I, A, C, and D show processive behavior |CITS: [10092840][10642523][10675564]|, whereas the other three enzymes (endopolygalacturonases II, B, and E) work via a single-attack mechanism |CITS: [10092840][10642523][10675564]|. All of the endo- and exo-polygalacturonases hydrolyze the substrate via an inverting mechanism and belong to the GH28 glycoside hydrolase family |CITS: [8605979]|.</Description>
